@title Ticketing Platform Architecture
@subtitle Tidewater Ferries, region North Europe, reviewed 2026-09-22
@author Digital Channels

# Overview

Passengers book through Azure Front Door. Every booking is written to one database and announced as an event so that the scanners at the terminals stay in step.

# Diagram

diagram tide dir=LR title="Ticketing on Azure"
tide: pax "Passengers" user
tide: fd "Front Door" lb
tide: web "Booking Web App" svc
tide: api "Ticketing API" svc
tide: rdb "Reservations DB" db "Azure SQL"
tide: bev "Booking Events" queue "Service Bus"
tide: scan "Port Terminal Scanners" ext
tide: pay "Payment Provider" ext
tide: pax -> fd : HTTPS
tide: fd -> web : origin request
tide: web -> api : REST
tide: api -> rdb : SQL, port 1433
tide: api -> pay : card authorization
tide: api -> bev : ticket issued
tide: bev -> scan : sync every 30 s
tide: group appsub "App subnet (10.50.1.0/24)" web, api
tide: group datasub "Data subnet (10.50.2.0/24)" rdb, bev
tide: group vnet "Ticketing VNet (10.50.0.0/16)" appsub, datasub
tide: group sub "Ticketing subscription" vnet

# Capacity

- **Booking Web App:** 3 to 10 instances at 60 percent CPU.
- **Ticketing API:** 4 to 16 pods.
- **Reservations DB:** 8 vCores, with a read replica in the paired region.

# Recovery targets

- Recovery point objective: 5 minutes.
- Recovery time objective: 1 hour.
- Last tested: 2026-08-19.
